	title = {Enhancing Ground Station Operations: An Automated Approach to Satellite Image Downlinking},
	year = {2024},
	journal = {Proceedings of the International Astronautical Congress, IAC},
	pages = {426 - 430},
	doi = {10.52202/078367-0045},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85218442563&doi=10.52202%2F078367-0045&partnerID=40&md5=5ff36b0419e1e2219c296a63936f3c8b},
	affiliations = {United Arab Emirates University, Al Ain, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {The pursuit of enhanced efficiency in Earth observation has led to the exploration of automation in satellite operations, particularly in the downlinking of satellite images. This paper presents a pioneering approach to automating this process, which traditionally requires substantial manual effort. Our study aims to mitigate the manual complexities by introducing an automated system that integrates seamlessly with ground station infrastructure. We developed an automation framework utilizing a combination of custom scripts and open-source tools, facilitating seamless communication through protocols and efficient image processing via a dedicated demodulator. Our methodology focuses on the synergy between these components to minimize human intervention and the associated risk of errors during the preliminary phases of downlinking. Our findings reveal that this automated approach significantly enhances the throughput of ground station operations, reducing manual errors and increasing the capacity to handle a wider array of satellite models. The flexibility and adaptability of the system were notable, underscoring its potential to accommodate the nuanced requirements of various satellites. In conclusion, automating the downlinking process of satellite images marks a significant advancement for ground stations, offering improvements in operational efficiency and precision. This system serves as a cornerstone for future integrations with sophisticated technologies such as machine learning, which could further revolutionise predictive maintenance and anomaly detection in satellite communications. The implications of this research underscore the transformative impact of automation on satellite-based Earth observation, setting the stage for a new era of enhanced responsiveness and agility. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Automation; Communication Protocols; Demodulator; Digital Signal Processing (DSP); Digitizer; Earth Observation; Ground Station; REST API; Satellite Image Downlinking},
