	doi = {10.1109/EIECS63941.2024.10800534},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85216317601&doi=10.1109%2FEIECS63941.2024.10800534&partnerID=40&md5=82da3149b41c492ec7b867f808f85ae4},
	affiliations = {Yantai University, School of Physics and Electronic Information, Yantai, China},
	abstract = {To address the issues of varying defect scales and background interference on steel surfaces, which result in low detection accuracy and slow speed in traditional YOLOv8 models, this paper proposes a new MGF -YOLO method for steel surface defect detection. By introducing the MobileNetV3-Small backbone network, the model's parameter count is reduced, enhancing computational speed. A new Dynamic-Ghost Conv (DGConv) is proposed by combining Dynamic Conv and Ghost Conv, improving the neck network's C2f module for more precise localization of fine textures and complex structural defects. Additionally, a weighted bidirectional feature pyramid network (BiFPN) is introduced to enhance the feature fusion network, reducing the probability of defect information loss and improving multi-scale feature recognition. To boost the accuracy and efficiency of detecting complex defects, a more focused Focaler-10 U loss function replaces the CIoU loss function, which improves convergence. Detection results on the NEU-DET dataset indicate that the MGF-YOLO model achieves an accuracy of 85.4%mAP@0.5 and 81.42 FPS, demonstrating a high balance between accuracy and speed. This makes it better suited for defect detection needs in steel strip production. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
